WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:06.300
It’s 1960 and look how happy Sharon,
and Karen, and Joey are.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.695
And Bob has completely forgotten the horrors
of the second World War.

00:00:13.251 --> 00:00:17.084
They are happy because of this glass door.

00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:19.300
But to understand doors 
like the ones sold here

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.314
you have to go to the patent application—

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:21.954
You know what?

00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:30.849
Because when Admiral Richard Byrd went to
Antarctica in 1939

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:33.894
Science News reported that 
he adorned his lab with

00:00:33.894 --> 00:00:36.292
non-frosting window panes.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.229
And newspapers around the country 
shared the picture.

00:00:41.229 --> 00:00:43.839
But this technology had a far greater impact

00:00:43.839 --> 00:00:47.181
than helping Arctic researchers 
study cute penguins.

00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:49.719
A new type of window enabled everything

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:53.510
from paradisal patios to a change in skyscrapers

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.154
from ones that look like this 
to ones that look like this.

00:00:57.154 --> 00:00:59.766
And in this case, the breakthrough had a name.

00:00:59.766 --> 00:01:02.433
Thermopane changed Bob’s world.

00:01:04.768 --> 00:01:08.982
I was just curious, what attracted you to
write about this and research this topic?

00:01:08.982 --> 00:01:12.400
When I've told people, oh yeah, I'm doing
a research project on insulated glass.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.214
You know, the response is always like, 
oh, what?

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:20.675
The reality is that it changed the way that
we did building enclosures, radically and

00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:23.228
in a very, very short period of time.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.412
The way that the building interiors were illuminated
for hundreds, thousands of years 

00:01:30.412 --> 00:01:31.450
was natural daylight.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.677
Or, you know, you had to burn something basically
to be able to see inside at night.

00:01:35.677 --> 00:01:40.588
When electricity came along in the 1870s,
1880s, that changes the formula.

00:01:40.588 --> 00:01:41.908
But not all the way, right.

00:01:41.908 --> 00:01:44.762
Electricity is still expensive for decades.

00:01:44.762 --> 00:01:48.568
And so you still have these very, very large
windows to bring daylight

00:01:48.568 --> 00:01:50.112
especially into offices.

00:01:50.112 --> 00:01:52.830
One thing I was reminded of, I did this 
one little series

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.250
on old movie studios, like in the silent era.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.870
And before they really got the lighting down

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.465
it's exactly what you're talking about because
they all look like greenhouses.

00:02:02.465 --> 00:02:06.209
Because they just have to adapt to the situation

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:09.083
of not being able to have powerful enoughlight.

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:13.569
That affects the architect’s aesthetic at
the time, is what you're saying.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:15.730
But there's a real environmental penalty there

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.680
because the glass is a pretty good insulator,
but the problem is

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.950
that it has to be so thin to see through

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.950
that you lose all of the heat in the building
during the winter

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.601
or you pick it all up in the summer.

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:32.100
So glass is both necessary, but also a problem.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.560
As electricity got cheaper and more places
started using heating and air conditioning

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.906
large windows kinda went out of style.

00:02:38.906 --> 00:02:44.589
In 1931, the Chicago Tribune wrote about architects
laughing at the idea of glass walls.

00:02:44.589 --> 00:02:49.584
They were “not feasible” because glass
was a “great conductor of heat and cold”.

00:02:49.584 --> 00:02:51.762
But people still wanted to see outside.

00:02:51.762 --> 00:02:56.010
Glass blocks like these were one solution
- they insulated well.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.861
As a plus, they offered privacy.

00:02:57.861 --> 00:03:01.597
But you couldn’t see out them clearly 
like real windows.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:06.864
So there has to be a wall

00:03:06.864 --> 00:03:09.251
between Bob and his children.

00:03:11.788 --> 00:03:13.349
Instead of watching them play

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:16.834
he’s forced to ponder the unspeakable things
he did during the war.

00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:23.275
But in 1934 Libby Owens Ford Glass Company

00:03:23.275 --> 00:03:26.417
annnounced a new glass process they acquired 

00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:29.339
from a refrigeration engineer 
in Milwaukee.

00:03:29.339 --> 00:03:35.490
This engineer realizes that if you can double
the glass, right.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.518
And between those two layers of glass,

00:03:37.518 --> 00:03:42.930
take out all of the humid air and replace it 
with dry air with all the water taken out.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.167
You can get not only the insulating value
of the two pieces of glass

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:51.154
but that thin layer of air in between 
does a really, really good job

00:03:51.154 --> 00:03:56.108
of preventing heat from jumping from 
one pane of glass to the next.

00:03:56.108 --> 00:03:59.530
Builders are chasing something called R-value.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.455
It’s a measure of insulation.

00:04:01.455 --> 00:04:03.633
Higher = more insulation.

00:04:03.633 --> 00:04:07.868
That pink insulation in your wall, 
it might have a really high R-value.

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:10.393
A pane of glass is down at 1.

00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:14.800
Thermopane doubled that at more than 2
and it was still transparent.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.109
Newspapers report that they 
extend the line and

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:21.675
soon that double pane glass 
was down in Antarctica.

00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:24.820
And Thermopane salesmen were claiming 
Admiral Byrd said

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.950
it kept his lab 75 degrees Fahrenheit 
behind the windows.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.309
Though the seal required some reworking 
over the next decade

00:04:32.309 --> 00:04:35.700
thermopane changed the possibilities for architects.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.139
Thermopane gets going, but then 
competitors do start up pretty quickly.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:44.060
So it becomes a competitive marketplace 
for  this type of dual system.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.030
Pittsburgh plate glass, which is one of their
big competitors

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.350
comes up with a slightly different way of
doing it.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.462
And so they patent that and they market it
as “Twindow”

00:04:53.462 --> 00:04:55.806
because it has two layers of glass.

00:04:55.806 --> 00:04:57.483
I would say by the 1960s,

00:04:57.483 --> 00:05:02.717
if you're putting a big window into any 
residential or office building

00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:04.940
other than, a very, very temperate climate,

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.445
you're probably just doing insulated glass
almost by default.

00:05:10.415 --> 00:05:13.870
Today, you can find triple pane windows 
and thin glass coatings

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.410
like “Low-E Glass” that have advanced
the same idea.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.041
They can bump up that R-value to above 3.

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:23.859
At the same time, builders worry about 
all the glass walls

00:05:23.859 --> 00:05:27.720
we’ll need to replace, often from seals
that are breaking down.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.457
But the Thermopane revolution really did change
how people work and live.

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:38.570
The residential advertising through this

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:45.330
was always a sliding glass door, looking out
onto very verdant landscape

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.332
which is basically saying, you know, here's
a way of connecting

00:05:48.332 --> 00:05:51.000
your inside world and your outside world.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.123
I noticed this blanket of air phrase 
a couple of times.

00:05:55.123 --> 00:05:59.910
Even though means very, very broad, wide open
glass window

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.479
you're sort of cuddled up in a blanket.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:09.000
"The family at home is enjoying the convenience
and the functional beauty of walls of glass."

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.251
"Merging room with room, blending inside with
outside."

00:06:13.251 --> 00:06:17.120
Taking away the barrier between the inside
and the outside

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.261
is kind of socially and culturally, what a
lot of these

00:06:21.261 --> 00:06:23.584
mid-century modern homes were all about.

00:06:23.584 --> 00:06:26.543
Being able to see all of your stuff.

00:06:26.543 --> 00:06:30.164
Thermopane was a way to 
stop staring at the past.

00:06:30.164 --> 00:06:31.301
And start seeing the future.

00:06:37.282 --> 00:06:40.229
I guess this is by Pittsburgh Plate Glass
Company too.

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:41.830
Is that kind of a different category?

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:47.900
Spandrel usually refers to the solid panel
between the transparent ones.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.599
And it does two things.

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:54.629
It covers up the edge of the floor plate,
which is usually

00:06:54.629 --> 00:06:58.850
you know, thick and kind of sloppy concrete.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:03.864
But for a long time — and this is a whole
other rabbit hole —

00:07:03.864 --> 00:07:07.080
but for a long time, these glass curtain walls

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:11.919
are running a foul of kind of old fashioned
fire codes

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:16.880
that say it needs to have a three foot, usually
three foot brick wall

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.759
between one window and the next, and so way
they got around that, like behind the dark

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:25.855
green panels in this building,

00:07:25.855 --> 00:07:31.768
there's probably a brick wall, but they're
putting a piece of opaque glass

00:07:31.768 --> 00:07:33.271
 in front of it

00:07:33.271 --> 00:07:37.587
to give you the illusion of an all glass facade

00:07:37.587 --> 00:07:42.204
Right, which was surprisingly like a fairly common
thing.

